## 1. The idea in one paragraph

An individual learning agent can pass every stability test we know how to
run and still help destabilize the market it operates in. When adaptive
agents share an environment that reacts to their decisions - dealers whose
quotes reshape a common pool of informed flow, recommenders competing for
one attention pool, pricers facing one demand curve - each agent's
retraining changes the data every *other* agent will learn from next. That
coupling is a **learning externality**: no agent internalizes the effect of
its own adaptation on the environment its competitors face, and the
feedback that is harmless in one agent compounds across many. We formalize
the externality, show that the number of interacting learners enters the
stability condition itself (individually stable agents with modulus
`m_1 < 1` destabilize the market at the critical population `N_c = 1/m_1`),
and then prove three things about *managing* it: a closed-form
**crowding-cadence frontier** (how much slower everyone must retrain as the
market gets more crowded, and the crowding level beyond which no cadence
helps), a **herd-immunity theorem** (the market is stable iff the fraction
of agents running feedback-aware corrected updates exceeds
`1 - N_c/N` - exactly the epidemiological `1 - 1/R_0` law, with the
systemic modulus as the reproduction number), and the **Pigouvian wedge**
that prices the externality. All three are validated in a structural
multi-dealer market simulator whose homogeneous predictions have already
been verified against theory (measured amplification `1.74x / 3.16x` at
`N = 2, 3` against predicted `2 / 3`).

